    \u0432\u0440\u0435\u043c\u044f \u043d\u0430 \u043e\u0444\u043e\u0440\u043c\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435
    \u0441\u0442\u0430\u0442\u0435\u0439."
  interestingFactsEn: "The life of Carl Friedrich Gauss is full of paradoxes: a genius who saw the laws of the universe, but couldn't find a common language with his own children; a punctuator who was afraid to publish drafts, but who made discoveries decades ahead; to systematize this fascinating legacy, I divided the facts into several categories: childhood and wonders, scientific curlies, home weirdness, relationships with colleagues and postmortem riddles. -- 1.Childhood: Genius in the cradle - The Father's Amendment at the age of three: The most famous legend states that when Father Gebhard counted the wages of the workers, a three-year-old Carl sat on the floor and played, suddenly said, \"Dad, the calculation is not true, there must be more.\" Father counted and, to his surprise, discovered a mistake.- Saving from poverty through mathematics: The teacher of Gaussa at the People's School, Buttner, once gave the task of folding the numbers from 1 to 100 in order to enter the class for a couple of hours. Gauss wrote the answer to the board instantly. Buttner, who was struck, said, \"This boy is not going to be a regular butcher!\" Buttner and his assistant Bartels convinced the Duke of Brownschweig to pay for Gauss' education.If it weren't for this random task, Gauss would probably have become a mason like his grandfather. Language talent: As a child, Gauss showed no less interest in philology than in mathematics. When he went to university, he hesitated for a long time between mathematics and classical languages (Latin and Greek). Even as a mathematician, he was free to read ancient writers in the original and kept his diary in Latin — in part so that colleagues could not read it. -- 2.Scientific Curioses: In advance of time for centuries - turning into \"The Prince of Mathematics\": Although we know him as a mathematician, his first world glory was... astronomical. When he counted the orbit of Cerera, French astronomer Joseph Laland said, \"This German found the planet sitting in a chair without looking at the telescope!\" That's what Princeps Mathematicorum called him, although the title refers to the whole math.- Military code: Gauss was so paranoid about privacy that he invented his own sophisticated code for personal correspondence with friends, and in the 1810s he suggested that the Hanover government use its encryption techniques for military correspondence, but officials found his ideas \"too complex to understand\".- An algorithm found 150 years later: In 1805, Gauss invented the Rapid Transformation algorithm Furier (BPF), which allows for the acceleration of signal processing. He used it for astronomical calculations, but considered it too trivial and did not publish it. When Kuli and Tuki \"reopened\" the BPF in 1965, it revolutionized digital processing (without it being JPEG, MP3 and cellular).The historians didn't find Goose's records until decades later.When young Hungarian Janos Boyai published his paper on non-euclidic geometry in the 1830s, Gauss wrote to him: \"To praise this work is to praise myself because I came to the same thing 30 years ago.\" Boyiai was crushed and never published any more mathematical works. --3: Domestic weirdness and weirdness (Ipochondria) -- Paranoia about toothache: Gauss was panicking dentists.He had terrible teeth, and when they started to get sick, he didn't go to the doctor, but invented his own mathematical methods of calculating the time of pain, and he carried with him a small notebook that recorded how many minutes a stroke lasted, and he took out the formula of \"periodicity\" of toothache, trying to predict the next attack, so that he could drink opium in advance.In his office, he maintained a temperature of not more than 14°C in winter, but hated cold legs, and he invented a system: sitting behind a desk with legs wrapped in several layers of wool blankets, while wearing a fur hat in the room. The students called it \"bear in the den.\" Mathematics before sleep: To sleep, Gauss did not read novels, but calculated simple numbers in his mind or tried to think about a certain theorem.In his letter to a friend, he admitted that at night he often had problems that he couldn't cope with during the day.Relationships with contemporarys: \"I am the mountain, they are the wind\" - Hate for \"unhelpful\" teaching: Gauss was a terrible lecturer. He read the material with a monotonous voice, often bubbling himself and making mistakes on the board. However, if a talented student (such as young Bernhard Riman) was sitting in the audience, Gauss became a radiant and began to shine his eyes and lecture so hard that other students could not understand.The war with Napoleon: Gauss hated the French after Napoleon's troops occupied Brownschweig and killed his patron, the Duke.- Competition with Legandr: Adrien-Marie Lejandr and Gauss almost simultaneously discovered the method of least squares. The Legendre published it first, but Gauss wrote in the foreword to his book that \"has used this method since 1794.\" The Legendr was pissed and accused Gauss of plagiarism, to which Gauss replied: \"If Lejandr cannot prove that my calculations of the Ceres were made later, let him shut up.\"They never made up, though Gauss was right -- his old diaries confirmed priority.Humboldt respected Gauss so much that he financed the construction of his magnetic observatory from his own pocket.- The theory of relativity in joke: When asked what he would do if he had found a solution to the number theory after 10 years, he said, \"I would have locked him in a safe for 10 years and wouldn't show him because math should be elegant immediately, not in a decade.\" - About \"dumble\" questions: once an official asked Gaussa if there was any practical benefit to the number theory. Gauss replied, \"This is the queen of mathematics. If you need practice, go to the blacksmith.\"Postmortem riddles and chickens - Wills and waxings: Gauss made a bequest that after his death his face and hands would remove his cast masks (this was the fashion of the time), but the servant, fearing that the scientist would wake up when the cast was poured, half ruined the mask, left only the blind eye of the right hand with the eternal pen, which is now stored in Göttingen.The brain of Gaussa: His brain was extracted by autopsy and weighed 1,492 grams — one of the hardest brains in history that far exceeded the average weight (about 1350 g). However, unlike Einstein's brain, Gauss' brain was not thoroughly examined — it was long stored in formalina, and it was not until 1998 that scientists proved that he had an unusually developed swirl that was responsible for spatial thinking.- The Cemetery Curse: On the grave of Gauss in Göttingen, not the formula or the portrait, but the golden five-point star (pentagram), but the funny thing is that the builders who set up the monument confused the drawing and cut out a pentagonal lens, not a flat star, a Gauss who, in his lifetime, mocked the calculations, would probably turn into a coffin, seeing this geometric inaccuracy.It wasn't until 2014 that the mistake was corrected by setting the right barrel.----the final characterization (in figures and facts) - In his life, Gauss published only 155 scientific works (five times smaller than that of Euler), but each of them discovered a new area. - He personally calculated several thousand orbits of asteroids and comets, doing it for \"rest\" from complex geometry. - He invented his diary, which he kept in Latin from 1796 to 1814. It had only 147 records, each containing the greatest discovery.When this diary was found in 1898, scientists awoke: Gauss beat 19th - century math for decades, but simply wrote it down as a note in the fields to avoid wasting time writing articles."
